"epic summary" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		&lt;epic-summary&gt;
		<header>The &lt;epic-summary&gt; element: the Overview's two-sentence summary, drawn as a lede.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph><text code>&lt;epic-summary&gt;</> is what the epic is, in two sentences, at the top of the Overview. It draws its text as a LEDE: the opening paragraph, a size up, in the headings' ink.</>

	<!-- no ui-list.spell-meta here:  any ui-item element on the page leaves epic-item unstyled (issue I9 of epic-components) -->
	<text class="meta" paragraph>
		Written for: anyone reading or writing an epic page's markup, or changing this element.

		Status: <label size="mini" color="green">built</>, in the <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-summary" link="/epic/src/components/epic-summary/"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-summary/" code>epic-summary</></> folder of the epics package.

		Its vocabulary (attributes, slots, parts, children): <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-summary-epicsummary-en-ts" link="/epic/src/components/epic-summary/EpicSummary.en.ts"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-summary/EpicSummary.en.ts" code>EpicSummary.en.ts</></>.

		The component: <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-summary-epicsummary-tsx" link="/epic/src/components/epic-summary/EpicSummary.tsx"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-summary/EpicSummary.tsx" code>EpicSummary.tsx</></>.
	</>

	<section label="1. Example">
		<icon slot="icon" name="eye"/>
		<example "Example">
			<html>
				<epic-summary>Plan docs drawn by Solid web components (<code>&lt;epic-page&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;epic-item&gt;</code> ...) in a new package, <a href="/spell-epic/" target="src-spell-epic"><code>packages/epics</code></a>, loaded by <code>&lt;ui-root&gt;</code> as a component pack, so a doc holds only its data and prose. The plan-doc tool moves into the package as TypeScript, and all 26 docs switch over at once at the end: a layout change becomes an element change, never a rewrite of every doc.</epic-summary>
			</html>
		</example>
		<text paragraph>This epic's own summary. On an epic page it sits first inside <text code>1. Overview</>.</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Markup">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<text paragraph>The template's summary:</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<epic-summary>Two sentences: what this plan does, and why.</epic-summary>
			```
		</>
		<text paragraph>From the tool's template (<link target="src-spell-epic-src-tool-templates-plan-html" link="/epic/src/tool/templates/plan.html"><text title="epic/src/tool/templates/plan.html" code>plan.html</></>): the session planning the epic replaces the placeholder. The text is plain text, or a <text code>&lt;p&gt;</>.</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Where it goes">
		<icon slot="icon" name="compass"/>

		<section label="Its parents" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Parent</>
					<cell>How</>
					<cell>How many</>
					<cell>As</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<link target="src-packages-epics-docs-epic-overview-html" link="/epic-overview">
							<text code>&lt;epic-overview&gt;</>
						</>
					</>
					<cell/>
					<cell>one at most</>
					<cell>The summary, two sentences.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>

		<section label="Its children" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Child</>
					<cell>Where</>
					<cell>How many</>
					<cell>What it is</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>prose</>
					<cell/>
					<cell>any number</>
					<cell>The summary.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="4. Attributes">
		<icon slot="icon" name="table columns"/>
		<text paragraph>None: it takes no attributes.</>

		<section label="Slots" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<text paragraph>Where its children show: a child goes in a slot by its <text code>slot</> attribute.</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Slot</>
					<cell>What goes in it</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>(default)</>
					<cell>The summary: text, or a <text code>&lt;p&gt;</>.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>

		<section label="Parts" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<text paragraph>Pieces of what it draws, which a page's CSS can style from outside with <text code>::part(name)</>.</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Part</>
					<cell>What it is</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>base</>
					</>
					<cell>The lede.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="5. How it looks">
		<icon slot="icon" name="palette"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>A paragraph in the body face, 17px, in the headings' ink, at most 72 characters wide.</>
			<item>The text stays the page's own: find-in-page and links in it work as anywhere else.</>
			<item>No colour, no fold: it shows whenever the Overview is open.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="6. Written by">
		<icon slot="icon" name="terminal"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><text code>epic create</>: the template's placeholder, <text code>Two sentences: what this plan does, and why.</></>
			<item>Then by hand, by the Claude session planning the epic: no command writes its text.</>
			<item><text code>epic convert</>'s second pass: an older doc's <text code>&lt;p slot="summary"&gt;</> became one.</>
		</>
	</>
	<html>
		<footer class="site-page-footer">
			<ui-divider></>
			<ui-text class="meta" paragraph>Last checked 2026-10-09 against branch <ui-text code>epic-components</>: the tables come from its vocabulary file, so they change with it.</>
